  {
   "id": "fVdT1yT0DQAJ",
   "from": "Sol Lago",
   "author": "7a0e942c35fdeb76",
   "date": "2015-12-25T11:49:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: standard error with updated ggplot2",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi!<div><br></div><div>Thanks for your response and the code! This might get the job done, but what I meant was whether someone knew a way of plotting standard error bars that does not inlvoving pre-averaging the data frame (to calculate the standard error) as a previous step to plotting. This used to be possible in previous versions of ggplot (see my sample code above) but it is no longer the case.</div><div><br></div><div>best wishes,</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Wednesday, December 23, 2015 at 8:19:54 PM UTC-3, Sol Lago wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there,<div><br></div><div>I am running into a problem since I updated ggplot2 to version 2.0.0. </div><div><br></div><div>Before, I normally calculated standard error bars using <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">stat_summary</font> with <b><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mean_cl_normal</font> </b>with <b><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mult=1</font> </b>(see <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19258460/standard-error-bars-using-stat-summary\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19258460/standard-error-bars-using-stat-summary&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792368382096000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Bg_vBs6Ybwuf9_RxQjWdK\">here</a> for rationale). However, since I updated ggplot2, this yields and error: </div><div><br></div><div><div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">ggplot(df, aes(x=condition, y=rt, fill=condition)) +</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  stat_summary(fun.y = mean, geom = &quot;bar&quot;, colour=&quot;black&quot;,size=.3) + </font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  stat_summary(fun.data = mean_cl_normal, mult = 1, geom = &quot;errorbar&quot;)</font></div><div style=\"color:rgb(255,0,0)\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div></div><div><font color=\"#ff0000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">Error in print(ggplot(df, aes(x = condition, y = amplitude, colour = condition)) +  : </font></div><div><font color=\"#ff0000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  error in evaluating the argument &#39;x&#39; in selecting a method for function &#39;print&#39;: Error: Unknown parameters: mult</font></div></div><div><br></div><div>Any ideas of what the problem is? And if setting <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mult</font> is no longer an option in ggplot2, do you know of any quick way of calculating error bars that does not involve pre-averaging my data frame before creating a plot? </div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a bunch!</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_nPM1mhaDgAJ",
   "from": "Pedro Aphalo",
   "author": "88a52045f19a8186",
   "date": "2015-12-26T19:03:28Z",
   "subject": "Re: standard error with updated ggplot2",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi!<div><br></div><div>mean_se is now defined in the ggplot2 source code and exported. </div><div><br></div><div>Have you tried this function with stat_summary?</div><div><br></div><div>The following modified example from the help for stat_summary works for me.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">d </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mtcars</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cyl</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mpg</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_point</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>d </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> stat_summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fun</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;mean_se&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> colour </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;red&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> size </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Cheers,</div><div><br></div><div>Pedro.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>On Friday, 25 December 2015 13:49:26 UTC+2, Sol Lago  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi!<div><br></div><div>Thanks for your response and the code! This might get the job done, but what I meant was whether someone knew a way of plotting standard error bars that does not inlvoving pre-averaging the data frame (to calculate the standard error) as a previous step to plotting. This used to be possible in previous versions of ggplot (see my sample code above) but it is no longer the case.</div><div><br></div><div>best wishes,<br><br>On Wednesday, December 23, 2015 at 8:19:54 PM UTC-3, Sol Lago wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there,<div><br></div><div>I am running into a problem since I updated ggplot2 to version 2.0.0. </div><div><br></div><div>Before, I normally calculated standard error bars using <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">stat_summary</font> with <b><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mean_cl_normal</font> </b>with <b><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mult=1</font> </b>(see <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19258460/standard-error-bars-using-stat-summary\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/19258460/standard-error-bars-using-stat-summary&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792368382098000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0ZE0wVkSjk6MyvSPWDv6Bi\">here</a> for rationale). However, since I updated ggplot2, this yields and error: </div><div><br></div><div><div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">ggplot(df, aes(x=condition, y=rt, fill=condition)) +</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  stat_summary(fun.y = mean, geom = &quot;bar&quot;, colour=&quot;black&quot;,size=.3) + </font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  stat_summary(fun.data = mean_cl_normal, mult = 1, geom = &quot;errorbar&quot;)</font></div><div style=\"color:rgb(255,0,0)\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div></div><div><font color=\"#ff0000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">Error in print(ggplot(df, aes(x = condition, y = amplitude, colour = condition)) +  : </font></div><div><font color=\"#ff0000\" face=\"courier new, monospace\">  error in evaluating the argument &#39;x&#39; in selecting a method for function &#39;print&#39;: Error: Unknown parameters: mult</font></div></div><div><br></div><div>Any ideas of what the problem is? And if setting <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">mult</font> is no longer an option in ggplot2, do you know of any quick way of calculating error bars that does not involve pre-averaging my data frame before creating a plot? </div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a bunch!</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
